    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Wadai was an Islamic sultanate in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chad">Chad</a>, founded in the early 17th century after a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tunjur">Tunjur</a>.</li><li>Its caravan road to Benghazi made it a major trans-Saharan trading power, fed largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> into the lands to the south.</li><li>Weakened by succession wars, it fell to the French, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abeche">Abéché</a> in 1909 and abolished the sultanate in 1912.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Anglo-French convention of 1899 assigned Wadai to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> to Britain, a division that still broadly underlies the Chad-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sudan">Sudan</a> border.</p></aside>
    </section>

